.TH BZ3CAT 1 "2026-10-21" "bzip3-rs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bz3cat \- decompress bzip3 files to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bz3cat
.RB [ \-e / \-z / \-d / \-t / \-r ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-j
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-B ]
.RI [ files... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bz3cat
is
.B bzip3
with decode-to-stdout defaults: it behaves as
.B "bzip3 \-dc"
unless explicit flags override it.
Every positional is an independent input; there is no
.I "in out"
form. With no files, standard input is decompressed to
standard output.
.B \-B
is accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.PP
Output always goes to standard output, so inputs are never
deleted and no output names are guessed.
.B \-r
selects recover mode: damaged blocks give their decoded bytes or zero bytes,
and the exit code is 2 when the tool found damage. See
.BR bzip3 (1).
.B \-\-rm
is rejected as an unknown flag.
.B \-f
is accepted but output files never apply; non-bzip3 bytes are
never passed through. Terminal refusal applies as in
.BR bzip3 .
.PP
.B \-q
suppresses non-critical warnings only; errors always print.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-j " " \fIN\fR ", " \-\-jobs=\fIN\fR
Worker thread count, 0 to 64. 0 (default) uses all available cores,
clamped to 64. 1 runs serially; 2 to 64 select that many workers.
Other flags match
.BR bzip3 (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, help, or version.
.TP
.B 1
Bad flags, unreadable or missing input, terminal refusal, or
allocation failure. With several files the worst per-file code
is returned.
.TP
.B 2
Corrupt data: bad magic, truncated input, bad header size,
inconsistent headers, block or CRC failure, or a trailing byte.
Recover mode also returns 2 when it found a damaged block or stopped early.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bzip3 (1),
.BR bunzip3 (1),
.BR bz3grep (1),
.BR bz3diff (1),
.BR bz3more (1)
